00:01:57.242 --> 00:01:59.630
(laughs)

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.463
I had no money.

00:02:00.463 --> 00:02:02.520
- [Gary] That's a really good answer.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.790
- I'd run out of money
in pursuit of trying to

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.860
raise money for people with disabilities

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.620
to have more access at big music venues

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.720
and I spent all of my money
trying to make that happen

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.580
and it didn't happen

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.930
because people are so
ableist in my country

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.910
and every country.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.150
But in particular, the
UK's got listed buildings

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.690
and they use that as a defense mechanism

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:21.794
to not update their buildings

00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:24.160
in order to be accessible
for people with disabilities

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.790
and so I lost all of my money doing that

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.790
and came to America with
no money and no exact plan.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:33.380
I wanted to be a writer.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:34.390
I had a pilot that I'd written,

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.676
I got signed to Three Arts
and they heard about, and UDA,

00:02:37.676 --> 00:02:41.620
they heard about this
audition, they sent me for it,

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:42.620
I was a big fan of Mike Schur.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.960
I never thought in a million
years I would actually get it

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:46.940
but when it came to the point where

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.120
I had to sign the seven year
deal before the final audition,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.660
they make you sign a seven year deal

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.180
before that final audition,

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:53.047
so all the power is

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:54.284
in their hands.
- [Gary] Of course.

00:02:54.284 --> 00:02:57.167
- And I needed the money and I was like,

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:59.609
"If I fail, I will fail rich."

00:02:59.609 --> 00:03:01.531
- [Gary] Yeah.
- Yeah, so you know.

00:03:01.531 --> 00:03:02.823
I'm into that.
- When was that?

00:03:02.823 --> 00:03:05.630
- That was three years ago.
- [Gary] Understood.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.090
- And now we've done four seasons,

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.030
it's a hit show, and
I didn't die on my ass

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.760
and I was able to do what
I wanted to do all along,

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.550
which was I've been an
activist since I was 19

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.020
and I've only ever entered show business

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.990
as a way to leverage my--
- [Gary] The awareness.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.073
- Awareness around things.
- [Gary] Of course.

00:03:20.073 --> 00:03:22.010
- I remember when I
was 19, I was watching,

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.920
that was around the
time that Angelina Jolie

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.230
was starting to raise awareness about

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.080
things that were happening
in Cambodia and Pakistan

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.780
and there are Cambodian
and Pakistani people

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.570
trying to raise awareness
and no one listens to them,

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:33.090
we only listen to the privileged.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.261
I was like, "Oh, that's nice."

00:03:35.261 --> 00:03:37.555
That's not nice, that's sad, but that's--

00:03:37.555 --> 00:03:39.260
- [Gary] That's the key.
- That's an effective way to

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.240
raise awareness is when you
have a lot of privilege,

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.073
then people listen to you.

00:03:42.073 --> 00:03:43.860
It's easier to raise
awareness from the inside

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.100
and so I got into this
industry in order to

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.640
be able to make the noise
that I am now able to make.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.850
- You know, it's super interesting,

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.000
the way, especially the reference point

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.400
with Angelina and prior to her,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.510
the thought of privilege.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.790
It's super interesting because

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:02.936
we're now living through
a very interesting time

00:04:02.936 --> 00:04:05.020
of the maturity of the internet itself,

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.930
which is social platforms, social networks

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.540
that have at such scale in our society

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.740
that in the past, privilege and wealth

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.440
and things of that nature
lead to awareness, right?

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.635
Lead to scale of people
paying attention to you.

00:04:19.635 --> 00:04:22.270
We're really living in
an interesting time where

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.480
the definition of that privilege

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.810
can almost get tweaked a little bit

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.120
because having sheer audience at scale,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.870
even before you've begun

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:35.160
or have started the process
of the monetization of that,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.140
can still create some of those effects

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.800
and so the attention becomes a trigger

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.660
to that leverage and privilege.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.790
That's what it used to be but

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.500
there were so many
gatekeepers in who became

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.370
those winning players,

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.370
whereas now, whether it's
TikTok or LinkedIn or YouTube.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.390
- The door's wide open.
- [Gary] It's just wide open.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.440
And the paradigm shifts
are going to be enormous.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.303
We're starting to see
significant nuances of that.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:03.240
Take me back a little further.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.240
A, I just it's interesting at 19

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.920
so I'm now very curious
about what happened,

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.053
or was it building?

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.670
Why at 19 did that happen?

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.670
Was it a cause that got you going?

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:15.830
Had it been subconsciously happening?

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.230
But even before we go there,

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:18.725
because I just love--
- [Jameela] You want to know

00:05:18.725 --> 00:05:19.558
about my birth?

00:05:19.558 --> 00:05:20.890
- I do, I very much.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.490
I really genuinely like it,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.240
you know I was born in the Soviet Union

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.470
so much of what everything I feel

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:28.470
has so much to do with the origin stuff

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.035
so yeah, I do like it.

00:05:30.035 --> 00:05:32.210
So where were you born?

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.050
What kind of kid were you?

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.370
Like what was your scene?

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.630
- I was in born in London.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.580
I was deaf as a child.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.680
- [Gary] Flat out?
- Flat out deaf.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.770
And then I would sometimes
have enough operations

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:42.980
to get my hearing back

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:44.000
and then my hearing would go again.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:44.880
I'd have another operation.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.900
I had seven major operations on my ears

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:49.070
before the age of 12.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.925
It was the final operation
on my 12th birthday,

00:05:50.925 --> 00:05:54.630
in which I managed to get
65% of my hearing back

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:57.570
so I have impaired hearing.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.700
But that was a big part of my childhood,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.873
which contributed to me
being a very quiet child,

00:06:01.873 --> 00:06:03.300
a very socially inept child.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.789
I was very starey because I would

00:06:04.789 --> 00:06:06.113
stare at people in order to take them in

00:06:06.113 --> 00:06:09.080
because I couldn't hear
them so I've maintained that

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.263
ability and that quality
and still freak people out.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.980
But it also made me a
hyper-observant person,

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.190
which has stood me in good stead

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.643
in this very dangerous industry.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.010
And in the world as a woman.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:28.730
So I was unpopular, spent
a lot of time on my own,

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:32.480
which allowed me to kind
of grow my creativity and

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.810
just sort of allow me to
become exactly who I was.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:36.833
I wasn't shaped by other
people around me because

00:06:36.833 --> 00:06:38.927
there were no other people around me.

00:06:38.927 --> 00:06:39.930
- You have siblings?

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.210
- I have a brother, he was older

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:43.430
but he went to live in Spain
when I was very, very young

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:44.630
so we were kind of separated

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.220
so I kind of lived as an
only child for many years.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.640
And I didn't come from any money

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.671
so parents were out working

00:06:52.671 --> 00:06:55.105
and they separated when
I was very, very young.

00:06:55.105 --> 00:06:59.800
And I went to a special
needs primary school

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:04.610
and then I got into, I got a
full scholarship into a school

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:06.120
that isn't for special needs kids

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.150
and during my time there
I regained my hearing.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.660
I was able to win a music scholarship

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.900
and an academic scholarship

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.830
and I just stayed there until I was 17.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:18.760
17, got hit by a car, broke
my back, didn't move for

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:19.593
about two years.
- How did that happen?

00:07:19.593 --> 00:07:21.590
- I was running away from a
bee that wasn't chasing me.

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.960
I just saw a bee and I
remembered Home Alone

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.520
and I ran away from it into traffic.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:27.387
- [Gary] Really?
- Yeah.

00:07:27.387 --> 00:07:28.800
- [Gary] Jesus.
- I run into traffic

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.570
when I see flying insects.

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.520
To me, that seems safer.
- [Gary] Understood.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.913
- So I'm an idiot.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:37.150
What's happening outside?

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:38.140
It's very lively, Jesus Christ.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.185
- Somebody thought it
was good idea to walk in

00:07:40.185 --> 00:07:41.860
in the middle of our podcast.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:42.693
- No it's fine, it's like Mardi Gras.

00:07:42.693 --> 00:07:46.040
- Shook my head and told
them it was not a good idea.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.030
He got the picture and
left and he now knows

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.290
he's going to get it.

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:52.540
I'm just kidding, I'm just kidding.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.575
So you had a bee, you decided
it was going to be problems,

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:57.551
it actually turned into a
massive fucking problem--

00:07:57.551 --> 00:07:59.310
- A bee changed the course
of the rest of my life

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:01.420
because that accident
was the single best thing

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:02.530
that ever happened to me.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.680
I recommend it to you all.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:05.650
- [Gary] Break your back.
- Yep.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.483
- [Gary] And lay down for two years.

00:08:06.483 --> 00:08:08.010
- It really humbles you
and there's something about

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.460
not being able to piss on your own

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:11.060
that really changes the way that you feel

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.060
about yourself and your body

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.070
and I think at that point,
when I was lucky enough

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.560
to come out of that still able to walk,

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.110
I realized that, god, I'd
really been mistreating my body

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.630
because up until then from
the age of 12 until 17

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.930
I was severely anorexic--
- [Gary] I'm sorry.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:25.760
- And starving myself all of the time

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.780
because it was the '90s, it
was all about heroin chic,

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.687
that was a term being used
non-ironically by grown adults

00:08:30.687 --> 00:08:32.720
who were fucking idiots

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.300
and they were putting that
out for very young kids

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.120
and the internet had just come up.

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:39.929
It was all these thinspiration websites.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:40.823
- [Gary] Of course, I remember.

00:08:40.823 --> 00:08:42.770
- I have ADD so you're gonna like--

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:43.640
